[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Homogeneous polishing and refinement boundary contracts

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SOLVER-POLISH-PRODUCTS-001
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Retain complex products and require coefficient dominance
STATEMENT: >>>
An eligible homogeneous row correction shall preserve every other complex
coefficient-times-state contribution and leave unrelated equations intact.
Rows with tied nonzero maximum coefficient magnitudes shall not be polished
under the existing epsilon-dominance criterion.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: dominant_sensor_row_reconstructs_every_small_complex_product uses
x0+r(1+j)x1=0 and x1=2-j, hence x0=-r(3+j);64generated dyadic gaps55..100,
Proptest seed2026111931. Independent all-row verification follows. The separate
equal_strength_rows_are_not_polished_without_dominance case remains unchanged
and independently fails the final verifier. This is private helper qualification.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SOLVER-CORRECTION-TERMINAL-001
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Stop after a malformed real correction response
STATEMENT: >>>
A real refinement response with an incorrect vector length shall fail with
NumericalFailure after its first provider callback rather than spending further
correction calls. A correctly shaped but unhelpful correction shall retain the
existing bounded three-callback refinement policy.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: malformed_real_corrections_stop_after_the_first_callback isolates
empty and overlong finite responses on a valid scalar identity system. A valid
zero correction supplies the control. No vendor factorization is simulated.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SOLVER-RESIDUAL-SCALE-001
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Preserve unavailable backward-error evidence on scale overflow
STATEMENT: >>>
The internal real backward-error helper shall return positive infinity when its
absolute residual scale overflows, even if signed products cancel to a finite
zero residual. A finite scale with an exact zero residual shall report zero.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Verification: finite_cancellation_cannot_hide_an_overflowed_backward_error_scale
uses A*x=(0,-1), x=(1,-1), and coefficients1e100 versus1e308. This preserves the
existing numerical policy; it does not change physical signal tolerances.
<<<
